];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-29", modified: "2026-09-29" });
const title = "6 Best Garage Door Bottom Seals for Drafts, Water and Mice";
const description =
  "Six garage door bottom and threshold seals compared, plus how to identify your retainer, measure the slot, and set the opener so the new seal actually closes.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "dgsl-t-style-garage-door-seal",
    "name": "DGSL Garage Door Bottom Seal T-Style Replacement",
    "category": "T-End Replacement Insert, 5/16-inch T",
    "badge": "Best Overall Replacement",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 2600,
    "pros": [
      "Fits the common 5/16-inch T-end retainer found on many steel sectional doors",
      "Flexible rubber rated by the seller for roughly -40°F to 140°F",
      "Slides into the existing retainer, so no new hardware or drilling is needed",
      "Cuts to length with a utility knife; check the listing for the length your door needs",
      "Inexpensive enough to replace before it cracks rather than after"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Will not fit 1/4-inch T, beaded, J-type or wood-door retainers — measure first",
      "Seller-brand product, so consistency between batches is not as well documented as OEM parts"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The straightforward fix for a steel door with a 5/16-inch T-end retainer and a cracked or flattened seal.",
    "description": "For most steel sectional doors, the right fix is not a kit but a new insert that slides into the retainer already on the door, and this is the one we would start with. DGSL makes a U-shaped rubber seal with 5/16-inch T-shaped ends, which matches one of the two most common T-end retainer sizes. You slide the old seal out, lubricate the channels, feed the new one in and trim it with a utility knife. The seller rates the rubber for roughly -40°F to 140°F, which is the range that matters for a seal that must stay soft in January and not turn tacky on a sun-heated driveway in August. The U-shape gives two layers of rubber against the floor, which closes small dips in the slab better than a single flat flap. The important caveat is fit. A 5/16-inch T will not lock into a 1/4-inch channel, and it will not work in beaded, J-type or double-L retainers, so measure the T on your old seal before ordering. Also check the seal width: the flattened width must suit the door thickness and the depth of any dips in the floor. If your door is a Clopay and you want a manufacturer part, Clopay sells its own replacement, which we cover below."
  },
  {
    "id": "tsunami-seal-garage-threshold",
    "name": "Tsunami Seal Garage Door Threshold Seal Kit",
    "category": "Floor-Mounted Threshold, Adhesive Kit",
    "badge": "Best Threshold Seal",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 2400,
    "pros": [
      "Adds a 1/2-inch raised barrier that stops wind-driven rain, leaves and snow at the floor",
      "Consumer kits include polyurethane construction adhesive and a caulk gun",
      "Available in lengths for single doors, double doors and contractor rolls",
      "Offered in gray, brown and black to blend with the floor or door",
      "Works with the existing bottom seal to close gaps on uneven slabs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "A permanent install: removal later means cutting and scraping cured adhesive",
      "Creates a small hump that some mowers, carts and rolling toolboxes feel"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best fix when water runs under the door or the slab is too uneven for any bottom seal alone.",
    "description": "A bottom seal can only follow the floor so far. When the slab has settled into a dip, slopes toward the garage or lets rainwater run under the door, a threshold on the floor is the better answer, and Tsunami Seal is the one we see recommended most often. It is a one-piece strip that bonds to the concrete with polyurethane construction adhesive and creates a 1/2-inch raised barrier. The door closes with its bottom seal just inside the threshold, so the two work together: the threshold blocks water and wind-blown debris, and the door seal presses against it to stop drafts. The consumer kits include adhesive cartridges and a metal caulk gun, and they come in lengths for single and double doors, with longer contractor rolls for wide or multiple doors. It is sold in gray, brown and black. Installation is simple but unforgiving. The concrete must be clean, dry and free of old coatings or oil where the adhesive goes, and you need to position the strip so the door closes just behind it. Close the door, mark the line, then glue and weight it overnight. Be aware that this is effectively permanent, and the 1/2-inch hump is noticeable under mowers and rolling carts. If you plan to coat or tile the floor, do that before installing the threshold."
  },
  {
    "id": "xcluder-x2-garage-door-seal",
    "name": "Xcluder X2 Rodent Proof Garage Door Seal Kit",
    "category": "Rodent-Resistant Seal + Retainer Kit",
    "badge": "Best Rodent-Proof Seal",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 400,
    "pros": [
      "Rubber gasket lined with a stainless steel screen and Xcluder stainless steel and poly-fiber fill fabric",
      "Secondary brush seal behind the gasket closes gaps on textured or uneven floors",
      "Complete kit with aluminum retainers, seal, brush and mounting screws",
      "Sold in kit lengths for single and double doors",
      "Aimed at the one pest problem ordinary rubber cannot solve: chewing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Premium price, several times that of a standard replacement insert",
      "Replaces your existing retainer rather than sliding into it, so installation takes longer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth the premium only if mice or rats are already chewing through your seal; otherwise a standard seal is enough.",
    "description": "Mice and rats get into garages two ways: through gaps, and by chewing through a rubber seal that is only a thin barrier. A new seal fixes the first problem but not the second, and Xcluder is built specifically for the second. The X2 kit uses a heavy-duty rubber gasket lined with a stainless steel screen and filled with Xcluder fabric, a blend of stainless steel and poly fiber that rodents cannot comfortably chew. Behind the gasket is a brush seal that conforms to textured or uneven concrete, closing the small gaps a solid rubber seal leaves and also blocking insects and light. Because it needs its own geometry, the X2 comes as a complete kit: aluminum retainers, the seal, the brush seal, screws and instructions. That means you are replacing the existing retainer rather than sliding a new insert into it, so allow a couple of hours and a drill. Kits are sold in lengths for single and double doors. Is it worth it? If you have found chewed rubber, droppings along the door edge or nests in the garage, yes: it addresses the actual cause. If your problem is drafts and water, a standard replacement seal and possibly a threshold will do the job at a fraction of the cost. Seal side gaps and wall penetrations too, or rodents will simply use those."
  },
  {
    "id": "clopay-garage-door-bottom-weatherseal",
    "name": "Clopay Replacement Garage Door Bottom Weatherseal",
    "category": "OEM Replacement Insert, 18 ft",
    "badge": "Best for Clopay Doors",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 700,
    "pros": [
      "Manufacturer part designed for Clopay doors and retainers",
      "18-foot length covers a standard double door with room to trim",
      "Cuts to length with a utility knife",
      "Avoids guessing between third-party T-end sizes on a Clopay door",
      "Widely stocked at Home Depot for same-day replacement"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Designed for Clopay retainers; other brands may need a different profile",
      "Sold mainly through Home Depot, so Amazon availability is limited and variable"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your door is a Clopay, the factory seal removes the guesswork about fit.",
    "description": "Clopay is one of the largest residential garage door makers in the United States, and its doors are common in newer homes. If you have one — the name is usually on a sticker on the inside of a door section or on the end stiles — the simplest replacement is Clopay's own bottom weatherseal. It is sold in an 18-foot length, enough for a standard double door with some left to trim, and cuts to length with a utility knife. The reason to pay for the OEM part is certainty. Third-party T-end seals are available in several T sizes, widths and hardnesses, and while many fit Clopay retainers, the factory part is designed for them. That matters more than it seems: a seal that is slightly too tight is almost impossible to pull through an 18-foot retainer, and one that is too loose can slide out as the door operates. The main drawback is availability. The Clopay seal is sold primarily through Home Depot, and Amazon listings are inconsistent, so check both before ordering. If your door is from another manufacturer, check that brand's parts listing first, then fall back to a universal T-end insert after measuring the old seal."
  },
  {
    "id": "md-87643-garage-door-bottom",
    "name": "M-D Building Products 87643 Aluminum & Vinyl Garage Door Bottom",
    "category": "Retainer + Seal Kit, 9 ft",
    "badge": "Best for Doors Without a Retainer",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 900,
    "pros": [
      "Includes an aluminum retainer and flexible vinyl insert in one kit",
      "Screws onto the bottom edge of wood doors or doors with a damaged retainer",
      "Aluminum base protects the door bottom from moisture and wear",
      "Replaceable insert, so the next seal replacement is easier",
      "Widely available from M-D, a long-established weatherstripping brand"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "9-foot length suits a single door; wider doors need a second kit or longer version",
      "Vinyl stiffens more than good rubber in hard freezes"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best way to give a wood door, or a door with a damaged retainer, a proper replaceable seal.",
    "description": "Many older and wood garage doors never had an aluminum retainer; the seal was simply nailed to the bottom rail. Others have a retainer that is bent, corroded or missing sections. For both, a retainer-plus-seal kit is a better long-term fix than nailing on another strip, and this M-D kit is a well-established option. It pairs an aluminum retainer that screws to the bottom edge of the door with a flexible vinyl insert. The aluminum also covers the door bottom, which is valuable on wood doors because that end grain is exactly where rot starts. The practical benefit is that the insert can be replaced later without removing hardware, so the next time the seal wears out it is a quick job. It is a 9-foot kit, which suits a single-car door; a double door needs a longer version or two kits joined carefully. The main limitation is the vinyl. It is durable and fine in most climates, but vinyl gets stiffer than EPDM rubber in sustained cold, so in areas with hard winters we would consider swapping in a rubber insert of the matching profile later. Before installing on a wood door, check the bottom rail for rot, and seal any bare wood with an exterior primer or paint first."
  },
  {
    "id": "frost-king-g9h-garage-door-seal",
    "name": "Frost King G9H Garage Door Bottom Seal Kit",
    "category": "Nail-On EPDM Rubber, 9 ft x 2-1/4 in",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 2000,
    "pros": [
      "Nail-on EPDM rubber that stays flexible in cold weather",
      "Channel shape compensates for moderately uneven floors and door bottoms",
      "2-1/4-inch width and 9-foot length suit single-car wood doors",
      "Nails and instructions included; installs with only a hammer",
      "One of the least expensive fixes on this list"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only for wood doors or wood bottom rails — it cannot be used with a steel door retainer",
      "Nails can loosen as the rubber flexes; stainless screws hold better"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "An inexpensive, effective seal for single wood doors that never had a retainer.",
    "description": "If your garage door is wood and has never had a retainer, the Frost King G9H is the easiest and least expensive way to seal it. It is a 9-foot length of 2-1/4-inch EPDM rubber that nails directly to the bottom of the door. EPDM is the right material for this: it stays flexible in cold weather and resists sunlight, unlike cheaper vinyl strips that stiffen and crack. The channel shape is designed to compensate for uneven concrete and door bottoms, and it handles the small dips found on most slabs. Installation needs only a hammer and the included nails. Remove the old seal, clean the door bottom, check it for rot, and seal any bare wood with exterior primer or paint. Close the door, position the seal so it compresses slightly against the floor, and fasten it from the center outward. Stainless steel screws with washers hold better than nails over time, since the constant flexing can work nails loose. The limitation is clear: it only works on a wood bottom rail. Steel doors with an aluminum retainer need a slide-in insert instead. For a double door, buy a longer version or two lengths and join them tightly at the center. As a budget fix for a single wood door, it is hard to beat."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Garage Door Bottom Seals",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-garage-door-bottom-seal/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know what size garage door bottom seal I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pull a few inches of the old seal from one end and check the edge shape. For T-end seals, measure the width of the T; 1/4 inch and 5/16 inch are common. For beaded seals, measure the bead diameter. Then lay the seal flat and measure its width, and measure the door width for the length."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are universal garage door seals really universal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only within a retainer type. Universal T-style seals fit T-end retainers of the stated size, but not beaded, J-type or double-L retainers, and a 5/16-inch T will not fit a 1/4-inch channel. Treat universal as meaning it fits many doors of one retainer type rather than any door."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I use a garage door threshold seal or a bottom seal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Start with a bottom seal if the existing one is worn, since it fixes most drafts. Add a threshold if water runs under the door, the floor near the door is uneven, or the gap is too large for a seal alone. In heated garages or where water is a problem, using both gives the best result."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a garage door threshold on an epoxy floor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but the adhesive bonds only as well as the coating bonds to the concrete. Many installers remove the coating where the threshold sits so the adhesive reaches bare concrete. Always clean and degrease the area thoroughly, and install the threshold after the floor coating has fully cured."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a garage door bottom seal last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many seals last several years, but lifespan depends on climate, sunlight and how often the door is used. Replace the seal when it is cracked, flattened, torn at the ends or no longer blocks light at the floor. EPDM rubber generally lasts longer than cheaper vinyl in cold and sunny climates."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do garage door seals keep mice out?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A new, well-fitted seal blocks the gaps mice use, which solves many problems. Standard rubber can be chewed, however, so if rodents are already gnawing through the seal, use a rodent-resistant seal like the Xcluder X2 with a steel mesh layer, and seal gaps at the corners, side seals and walls too."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my garage door reverse after installing a new seal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A thicker seal can make the door touch the floor sooner than the opener expects, and the force setting may cause it to reverse. Adjust the close limit following your opener manual so the seal is lightly compressed, then test the safety reversal with a board on the floor to confirm the door still reverses properly."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best garage door bottom seal",
      "best garage door threshold seal",
      "garage door weather stripping",
      "rodent proof garage door seal",
      "T-style garage door seal",
      "garage door seal replacement"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            6 Seals Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Garage Door Bottom Seals</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Most garage door seal problems come from buying the wrong profile for the retainer. Identify your retainer first, then choose between a replacement insert, a floor threshold or both.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#dgsl-t-style-garage-door-seal"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Identify Your Retainer
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#bottom-or-threshold" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Bottom Seal, Threshold or Both?</a>
          <a href="#retainer-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Identify Your Retainer Before You Buy</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#seal-profiles" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Bulb vs T-Style vs Beaded Seals</a>
          <a href="#threshold" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Threshold Seals for Uneven Floors and Water</a>
          <a href="#rodents" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Rodent-Resistant Seals</a>
          <a href="#cold-weather" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cold Weather: Rubber vs Vinyl</a>
          <a href="#install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Installing a Replacement Seal</a>
          <a href="#close-limit" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Adjusting the Opener's Close Limit</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A garage door bottom seal is not a universal part. The rubber has to match the channel it slides into, the floor has to be flat enough for it to close against, and the opener has to push it down far enough to compress. Get those three right and a modest seal will stop drafts, water and most pests. Get one wrong and even the best seal leaves daylight under the door.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="bottom-or-threshold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Bottom Seal, Threshold or Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A garage door has two possible seals at the floor. The bottom seal is attached to the door and moves with it. The threshold seal is fixed to the concrete and stays there. They solve different problems, and many garages need only one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the bottom seal. If the rubber on the door is cracked, flattened, torn at the ends or missing sections, replacing it fixes most drafts and gaps. It is the cheapest and most common repair, and on steel doors it is a slide-in job with no drilling or adhesive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add a threshold when the problem is water or the floor. If rain runs under the door, if the slab has sunk near the door or slopes inward, or if the gap under the door is larger than a new bottom seal can close, a raised threshold is the practical fix. It creates a small barrier on the floor for the door seal to close against.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use both when you need the best seal possible. That combination makes the most sense in a garage you heat, a garage that doubles as a workshop, or one where the garage floor is finished. If you run one of the heaters in our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-heater/">best garage heaters</a>, a good seal matters as much as the heater, because warm air escapes fast under a leaking door. Similarly, a garage floor finished with <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">epoxy</a> or <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">interlocking tiles</a> benefits from keeping rainwater and road salt off it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DAYLIGHT TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Close the door on a sunny day, turn off the lights and look along the floor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Light along the whole length means a worn or wrong seal. Light only at one spot means an uneven floor, a door out of level or an opener that stops too high — and a new seal alone may not fix it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="retainer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Identify Your Retainer Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            On most steel sectional doors, the seal slides into an aluminum channel, called the retainer, fixed to the bottom of the door. The retainer shape decides which seals fit, and buying the wrong profile is the most common mistake in this category.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Retainer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you see</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Seal edge</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to measure</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">T-end (T-style)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two parallel slots with a flat groove inside</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat T-shaped edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">T width, commonly 1/4 in or 5/16 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Beaded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two slots with round grooves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Round beads</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bead diameter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">J-type</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Single channel with hooked lips</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">J-shaped hooks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Channel width and profile</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Double-L</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two L-shaped angles facing each other</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat or T edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gap between the Ls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">None (wood door)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seal nailed or screwed to bottom rail</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat flange</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Door thickness</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The easiest way to identify your retainer is to open the door to waist height, pull a few inches of the old seal out from one end and look at its edge. A flat T-shape means a T-end retainer. A rounded bead means a beaded retainer. A hooked edge means a J-type channel. If the rubber is nailed directly to wood, there is no retainer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then measure precisely. For T-end seals, measure across the flat T with calipers or a ruler; the two common sizes are 1/4 inch and 5/16 inch, and the wrong size either will not slide in or will pull out. For beaded seals, measure the bead diameter. Then lay the old seal flat and measure its total width, because seals are sold in widths such as 3, 4 and 6 inches, and the right width depends on door thickness and the size of the gap you need to close.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the old seal is missing or unrecognizable, check the door maker's parts listing using the name on the door label. If the retainer itself is bent or corroded, replace it with a retainer-and-seal kit rather than forcing a new seal into a damaged channel.
          </p>
        </div>

