        <div id="seal-profiles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Bulb vs T-Style vs Beaded Seals
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know the retainer, the seal profile is mostly decided, but the shape of the part that touches the floor still matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            T-style and beaded seals are usually U-shaped or loop-shaped. Both edges lock into the retainer and the rubber forms a loop under the door. When the door closes, the loop flattens against the floor and conforms to small dips. The difference between them is only the edge that goes into the retainer: T-shaped for T-end channels, round for beaded channels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bulb seals have a hollow round or oval section, and are common on wood doors and on some retainer designs. The hollow bulb compresses easily, which makes it good at sealing against slightly uneven concrete with little closing force. The trade-off is durability: a bulb can be split by ice, grit or a door that closes too hard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flat or fin-style seals are found on nail-on strips and some J-type systems. They are simple and cheap, but they seal only where they touch and handle uneven floors less well than a loop or bulb.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width matters as much as profile. A wider loop can close a bigger gap, but too wide a seal bunches up when compressed and can push the door back open with some openers. Match the width of the old seal unless it was clearly too narrow for the gap. If you need to close more than about half an inch at the low spot, consider adding a threshold instead of simply choosing a larger seal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="threshold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Threshold Seals for Uneven Floors and Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            A threshold seal is a raised strip bonded to the garage floor, typically about half an inch tall. The door closes just inside it, so the bottom seal presses against the strip and the strip blocks water and debris from coming in at floor level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thresholds solve two problems a bottom seal cannot. First, water. If your driveway slopes toward the garage, rainwater running down it will pass under even a perfect bottom seal, while a threshold creates a dam. Second, uneven floors. Concrete slabs often settle or crack near the door, and a threshold gives the seal a consistent surface to close against.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement is important. Close the door and mark a line along the outside face of the bottom seal. The threshold goes just inside that line so the door lands on the outer edge of the strip. Too far in and the door lands on top of the threshold and may not close properly; too far out and the seal does not touch it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adhesion depends on preparation. Clean the concrete thoroughly, degrease any oil stains and make sure it is dry. Old paint or coatings under the adhesive will limit the bond to the strength of the coating, so remove them where the threshold goes. Use the adhesive supplied or recommended by the threshold maker, press the strip in firmly and let it cure before driving over it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thresholds are best installed after any floor coating or flooring. See our guide to <a href="/garage-flooring-options/">garage flooring options</a> if you are planning a new floor, and seal the joint between the slab and the driveway, as well as gaps where the door jambs meet the floor, with a flexible <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rodents" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Rodent-Resistant Seals
          </h2>

          <p>
            A mouse can fit through a gap about the size of a pencil, and garage doors are one of the most common entry points. An old seal with split ends or a gap at the corners is an easy way in, and rodents will also chew through soft rubber to enlarge an opening.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first step is a proper fit. Replace worn seals, make sure the door closes fully, and close the corners where the bottom seal meets the side seals. Many rodent problems are at the corners rather than along the length of the door.
          </p>

          <p>
            If rodents are actively chewing through rubber, a rodent-resistant seal is the answer. The Xcluder X2 kit adds a stainless steel screen and a steel-wool style fill fabric inside the rubber seal, plus a brush seal behind it. Chewing through steel is unpleasant enough that rodents give up and look elsewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for signs before spending money: chewed seal edges, droppings along the inside of the door, nesting material in corners, or gnawed boxes stored near the floor. If you see those, a rodent-proof seal is worth the cost. If not, a standard seal and good housekeeping are usually enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember the rest of the garage. Rodents also use gaps around the side and top seals, holes where pipes and cables pass through walls, and gaps under the door into the house. Fill small gaps with exterior caulk or copper mesh, and keep bird seed, grass seed and pet food in sealed containers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cold Weather: Rubber vs Vinyl
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seal material matters most in cold climates. A seal only works if it stays soft enough to conform to the floor, and many cheaper materials stiffen as temperatures drop.
          </p>

          <p>
            EPDM rubber is the standard for durable seals. It stays flexible in cold weather, resists ultraviolet light and ozone, and does not become sticky in summer heat. When a manufacturer states a temperature range, look for one that reaches well below your coldest winter nights.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinyl, PVC and similar plastics are cheaper and often used in replacement inserts and thresholds. They work well in moderate climates and resist moisture, but they stiffen more in severe cold. A vinyl seal that is too stiff can hold the door slightly above the floor or crack when the door closes on ice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frozen seals are a separate problem. In freezing weather, water trapped under the door can freeze the seal to the floor, and when the opener lifts the door the seal can tear. Keep the area in front of the door clear of snow and slush, and apply a silicone spray to the seal before winter to help prevent it from sticking.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you heat your garage, invest in a better seal. The temperature difference between inside and outside drives air through every gap, and a stiff or cracked seal can cancel much of the benefit of insulation or a heater.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installing a Replacement Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing a slide-in seal on a steel door takes about an hour with two people. You need a utility knife, a silicone spray or dish soap solution, pliers, a flat screwdriver and possibly a helper to feed the seal while the other pulls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Open the door to about waist height and unplug the opener so it cannot move. On some doors, the retainer ends are crimped or held with a screw to keep the seal in place, so open them carefully with pliers or remove the screw. Pull the old seal out from one end. If it is stuck, spray the channel with silicone and pull steadily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the retainer channels with a rag and check them for bent sections, which can be straightened carefully with pliers. Then lubricate the channels and the edges of the new seal generously. Silicone spray or a soapy water solution makes a huge difference; without it, a long seal can be almost impossible to pull through the retainer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feed both edges of the new seal into the channels at the same time and slide it along the door. One person feeds and keeps the seal straight while the other pulls from the opposite end. Leave an extra inch or two at each end, then close the door to let the seal settle before trimming it flush with the door edge. Crimp the retainer ends or refit the screws so the seal cannot slide out.
          </p>

          <p>
            On wood doors without a retainer, remove the old strip, check for rot, and prime and paint any bare wood before fitting the new seal. Our guide to the <a href="/best-primer/">best primer</a> covers exterior-rated primers for bare wood. Fasten the seal from the center outward with stainless screws or nails.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="close-limit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Adjusting the Opener's Close Limit
          </h2>

          <p>
            A new seal is often thicker than the worn seal it replaces. If the garage door opener is still set for the old seal, the door may stop too high and leave a gap, or push too hard and reverse. Adjusting the close limit is part of the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The close limit tells the opener where the door stops when closing. Older openers use adjustment screws on the motor unit, while newer models use buttons and a learning procedure. Check the opener manual for your model, because the method varies.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is for the door to close with the seal lightly compressed along its length. Close the door and look at the seal: it should flatten slightly and touch the floor all the way across, without being crushed flat or bowing the bottom panel. Make small adjustments and test after each one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always test the safety reversal after changing the close limit. Place a 1.5-inch-thick piece of wood, or a 2x4 laid flat, on the floor under the center of the door and close it. The door should reverse when it touches the board. If it does not, adjust the force or limit settings according to the manual before using the door again.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">QUICK REFERENCE</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/4 or 5/16</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common T-end sizes in inches</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/2 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical threshold height</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.5 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Reversal test board thickness</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical slide-in seal swap</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the door still shows light on one side after adjustment, the problem may be the floor or the door. A threshold can fix a sunken slab, but a door that is out of level or has broken springs or cables needs attention from a garage door technician. Do not try to adjust springs yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a seal before measuring the old one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                T-end seals come in more than one size, and beaded, J-type and double-L retainers each need their own profile. A universal seal that does not match your retainer will not slide in or will pull out. Pull out the end of the old seal, measure the T or bead, and match it before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing without lubricant</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new seal has to slide the full width of the door through a tight aluminum channel. Without silicone spray or soapy water, the friction can make it impossible to pull through, and people often tear the seal or bend the retainer trying. Lubricate the channels and the seal edges generously before feeding it in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a wider seal to close a big gap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oversized seal bunches when compressed and can make some openers reverse or leave the door stopped just short of closing. If the gap is larger than your seal can close, check the floor, door level and close limit, and consider a threshold rather than simply going wider.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to adjust the opener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new seal is usually thicker than the worn one. Without adjusting the close limit, the door can stop high and leave a gap or press too hard and reverse. Adjust the limit, check that the seal is lightly compressed across its length, and always retest the safety reversal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Placing a threshold without closing the door first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A threshold that is too far inside ends up under the door, which can stop it closing fully or cause the opener to reverse. Too far out and the seal misses it. Close the door, mark the outside face of the bottom seal, and set the threshold just inside that line.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know what size garage door bottom seal I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pull a few inches of the old seal from one end and check the edge shape. For T-end seals, measure the width of the T; 1/4 inch and 5/16 inch are common. For beaded seals, measure the bead diameter. Then lay the seal flat and measure its width, and measure the door width for the length.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are universal garage door seals really universal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only within a retainer type. Universal T-style seals fit T-end retainers of the stated size, but not beaded, J-type or double-L retainers, and a 5/16-inch T will not fit a 1/4-inch channel. Treat universal as meaning it fits many doors of one retainer type rather than any door.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a garage door threshold seal or a bottom seal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with a bottom seal if the existing one is worn, since it fixes most drafts. Add a threshold if water runs under the door, the floor near the door is uneven, or the gap is too large for a seal alone. In heated garages or where water is a problem, using both gives the best result.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a garage door threshold on an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but the adhesive bonds only as well as the coating bonds to the concrete. Many installers remove the coating where the threshold sits so the adhesive reaches bare concrete. Always clean and degrease the area thoroughly, and install the threshold after the floor coating has fully cured.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a garage door bottom seal last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many seals last several years, but lifespan depends on climate, sunlight and how often the door is used. Replace the seal when it is cracked, flattened, torn at the ends or no longer blocks light at the floor. EPDM rubber generally lasts longer than cheaper vinyl in cold and sunny climates.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do garage door seals keep mice out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A new, well-fitted seal blocks the gaps mice use, which solves many problems. Standard rubber can be chewed, however, so if rodents are already gnawing through the seal, use a rodent-resistant seal like the Xcluder X2 with a steel mesh layer, and seal gaps at the corners, side seals and walls too.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my garage door reverse after installing a new seal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A thicker seal can make the door touch the floor sooner than the opener expects, and the force setting may cause it to reverse. Adjust the close limit following your opener manual so the seal is lightly compressed, then test the safety reversal with a board on the floor to confirm the door still reverses properly.
              </p>
            </div>

